7-a6 (one square). Similarly for b7.\n\nWait, the valid moves list includes P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6. So those pawns can move one or two squares. Since they're on the 7th rank (for Black, which is the second rank from the top), moving forward would be towards lower ranks. Wait, for Black, pawns move down the board (since White is at the bottom). So a7 is the starting position for Black's a-pawn (since Black's pawns start on rank 7). So moving from a7 to a6 is one square forward, a7 to a5 is two squares.\n\nIn rank 3: there's a KWF1 at b3. Wait, KWF1 is White King? Wait no, K is King, W is White. So White king is on b3? That's very advanced. And also a PBF1 at h3. Wait PBF1: Black Pawn, has moved (Moved 1), 2Step F (so didn't move two squares last turn). Wait but the pawn is on h3. Wait, how did a Black pawn get to h3? Because Black pawns move downward (from rank 7 to 1), so h3 would be three ranks down from h7. Wait, but h3 is rank 3, which is closer to White's side. Wait, maybe the board is represented with White at the bottom (ranks 1-2), Black at top (7-8). So for Black, moving down the board (toward rank 1) is forward. So a Black pawn on h3 would have moved from h7 to h3, which is four squares down. But pawns can only move one or two on first move. Wait, this seems odd. Wait, maybe there's a mistake here.\n\nWait, looking at the board:\n\nAt position h3 (file h, rank 3), it's PBF1. So Black pawn that has moved (so Moved=1), and 2Step=F (so last move was not two squares). But how did it get to h3? Because Black's pawns start on rank 7. So moving from h7 to h3 would require four moves. But pawns can only move one or two on first move, then one each time. So h7 -> h6 (move 1), h6->h5 (move 2), h5->h4 (move3), h4->h3 (move4). So yes, possible. So that pawn has moved four times, hence Moved=1.\n\nBut in the current state, the White king is on b3 (KWF1: White King, has moved). That's very far advanced. So White king is deep into Black's territory. That seems dangerous for White, but maybe it's a trap.\n\nWait, but the Black king is on g8. So White king is at b3, which is on the queenside, while Black king is on g8 (kingside). So perhaps White has been moving their king aggressively.\n\nNow, the valid moves for Black are:\n\nP: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, K: g8-f7, K: g8-f8, K: g8-g7, K: g8-h7, K: g8-h8, P: h3-h2.\n\nWait, the h3 pawn is Black's (PBF1), so moving h3 to h2. Since Black pawns move downward (toward rank 1), h3 to h2 is forward. Wait, but for Black, moving forward is decreasing rank numbers. So h3 to h2 is one square forward. But why would you move a pawn backward? Wait no: for Black, pawns move from rank 7 to 1, so direction is downward. So h3 is rank 3, moving to h2 (rank 2) is forward. So that's a valid pawn move.\n\nBut why is there a Black pawn on h3? Maybe it's part of some attack.\n\nNow, the main thing to consider is the position of the White king on b3. It's very exposed. If Black can attack it, maybe checkmate is possible.\n\nBut let's check the current position.\n\nBlack's pieces:\n\n- King on g8.\n\n- Pawns on a7, b7 (both unmoved, since their Moved flag is 0 in the board state: PBF0), and h3 (moved).\n\nWhite's pieces:\n\n- King on b3.\n\nWait, are there any other White pieces? The board shows only KWF1 at b3 and PBF1 at h3 (but h3 is Black's pawn). Wait, the board representation for rank 3 is:\n\n3 |    |KWF1|    |    |    |    |    |PBF1| 3\n\nSo position b3 is KWF1 (White King), and h3 is PBF1 (Black Pawn). So White has only their king on b3, and Black has pawns on a7, b7, h3, and king on g8.\n\nWait, that can't be right. Because in a normal chess game, each side has more pieces. But according to the given board state, maybe it's a simplified scenario. The problem states that the current state is as per the board given. So perhaps it's an endgame with only these pieces left.\n\nSo White has only their king on b3, and Black has king on g8, pawns on a7, b7, h3.\n\nWait, but h3 is 