 the new queen can attack the White king.\n\nBut the Black king is on g8, which is far away. So Black needs to either use the pawns to create a passed pawn or use the king to support.\n\nAnother angle: is the White king in danger of being checkmated? Let's see. The White king is on b3. If Black can get their king to, say, d4, but that's far. Alternatively, if Black promotes a pawn to a queen, then Q + K might be able to checkmate.\n\nBut with the Black king so far, maybe the best plan is to push the a and b pawns to promote.\n\nLooking at the valid moves:\n\nOption 1: Push a7 to a5. This is a two-square move, which might be better as it gets the pawn further advanced. However, after a7-a5, the pawn is on a5. Then White's king might capture it if possible. Wait, but White only has a king. So White king could move to a4, then capture the pawn on a5 if it's unprotected. But Black's pawn on a5 would be protected by the b7 pawn? No, because pawns protect diagonally. The a5 pawn would be protected by a pawn on b6, but currently, the b7 pawn is still there.\n\nWait, if Black plays a7-a5, then the pawn is on a5. The White king on b3 could go to a4 (next move) and then capture the pawn on a5 if it's undefended. But Black's b7 pawn could support it if advanced.\n\nAlternatively, pushing b7 to b5 might be better, as it's closer to the center.\n\nBut let's think about which pawn is more likely to promote. The a and b files are on the queenside, where the White king is located. The White king is on b3, so it's in the area of the a and b pawns. So the White king can potentially stop those pawns from promoting by capturing them or blocking.\n\nThe h3 pawn is on the kingside. The White king is on b3, which is far from h2. So advancing h3 to h2 might be safer, as the White king would take many moves to get over there.\n\nFor example, if Black plays h3-h2, then next move h2-h1=Q, promoting to a queen. Then Black would have a queen and king against White's lone king, which is a forced