ample:\n\n1... b5-b4\n\nWhite king moves a2-b3 (heading towards the b-pawn).\n\nBlack can then push a5-a4.\n\nWhite king b3-c4, etc.\n\nBut the Black king is on g8, which is very far. So unless the Black king can come into play, the White king might stop both pawns.\n\nBut given that the Black king is on g8, it\'s 6 files away (g to a is 6 files) and 6 ranks (8 to 2 is 6 ranks). So it would take the Black king many moves to get to the action. Meanwhile, White\'s king is already near the pawns.\n\nSo perhaps the best chance is to push the pawn that\'s further from the White king, i.e., the b-pawn.\n\nBecause the White king is on a2, the b-pawn on b5 is one file away. Pushing b5 to b4 forces White to decide which pawn to go after.\n\nIf Black pushes b5-b4, then the White king has to move towards it. Let\'s see:\n\nAfter ...b4, White\'s best move is probably K moves towards b4, like a2-b3.\n\nThen Black can push a5-a4, and now White has two pawns advancing. But White\'s king can only handle one.\n\nWait, but with two pawns, sometimes you can create a passed pawn.\n\nAlternatively, maybe advancing both pawns in a way that the king can\'t stop both.\n\nBut given the current position, let\'s see the distance.\n\nFor the b-pawn: from b5 to b1 needs 4 moves (b5-b4-b3-b2-b1).\n\nWhite king on a2. To reach b1, the king needs to move from a2 to b1: that\'s 2 moves (a2-b2-b1). But the pawn needs 4 moves. So if the pawn moves b5-b4 (1), king moves a2-b3 (1). Pawn b4-b3 (2), king b3-c4 (2). Wait, no, the king would move towards the pawn.\n\nWait, maybe using the rule of the square for the b-pawn.\n\nThe pawn is on b5. The square would be b5 to b1 and f5 to f1 (assuming square size of 4). The White king is on a2. Is a2 inside the square of the b-pawn?\n\nThe square for b5 pawn: the square extends from b5 to b1 and (b+4)=f5 to f1. So the square\'s leftmost file is b, rightmost f. The king on a2 is outside the square (since a is left of b), so the pawn can promote if it advances without the king\'s help.\n\nWait, the rule of the square says that if the opposing king is outside the square, the pawn can promote.\n\nFor a pawn on b5, the square is a 4x4 square (since it needs 4 moves to promote). The square would have corners at b5, b1, f5, f1. Wait, no: the square is such that the side length equals the number of moves the pawn needs to promote. From b5 to b1 is 4 ranks, so the square would be 4 files wide. So the square starts at b5, goes left 4 files to (b-4)= -2 (which doesn\'t make sense), but usually, it\'s the distance to promotion.\n\nActually, the rule is: the square is formed by the pawn\'s current square, the promotion square, and the squares in between. The king must be able to enter this square to stop the pawn.\n\nAlternatively, the number of moves the pawn needs to promote is (current rank - 1) for Black (since Black promotes at rank 1). So pawn on rank 5 needs 4 moves (5→4→3→2→1).\n\nThe king\'s distance to the promotion path: the minimum number of moves the king needs to reach the pawn\'s file on the promotion path.\n\nFor the b-pawn on b5:\n\nPromotion path is b-file. White king on a2.\n\nThe king needs to reach b-file before the pawn promotes.\n\nNumber of moves for pawn: 4.\n\nNumber of moves for king to reach b-file: from a2 to b1 is 2 moves (a2→b2→b1), but the pawn would have moved 2 times by then.\n\nWait, let\'s count:\n\nMove 1: Black pawn b5→b4. Now pawn needs 3 more moves.\n\nWhite king a2→b3 (1 move).\n\nMove 2: Black pawn b4→b3. Needs 2 more.\n\nWhite king b3→b2 (2 moves).\n\nMove 3: Black pawn b3→b2. Needs 1 more.\n\nWhite king b2→b1 (3 moves).\n\nMove 4: Black pawn b2→b1 (promotes).\n\nBut White king reaches b1 on move 3, so when Black plays b2→b1, the king is on b1, so the pawn is captured? Wait, no: when the pawn moves to b1, it promotes, and the king is on b1, so the pawn would be capturing the king, which isn\'t possible. Wait, no: the king can\'t be on b1 when the pawn promotes because the pawn\'s promotion wou