pawn:\n\nAfter 1...b4 2. Kb3 a4 3. Kxa4\n\nThen Black has the b-pawn on b4, but White king is on a4. Then Black can play b4-b3, and White king has to move back.\n\nBut it\'s messy.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the best move is to advance the pawn that\'s further from the White king, i.e., the b-pawn, to create a passed pawn.\n\nAnother angle: the Black king is on g8. Maybe the king needs to come into play. But it\'s very far. The valid king moves are to f7, f8, g7, h7, h8. Moving the king towards the center or towards the pawns might help in the long run.\n\nFor example, moving Kg8-f7 brings the king one step closer to the center. But given that the White king is already near the pawns, it might take too long for the Black king to arrive.\n\nIn pawn endgames, often the side with the pawns wants to advance them quickly before the opponent\'s king can stop them.\n\nGiven that, pushing the b-pawn (b5-b4) might be better than the a-pawn because the White king has to move one extra file to reach it.\n\nLet\'s compare the two pawn moves:\n\nOption 1: a5-a4.\n\n- White responds with Ka3.\n\n- Now a-pawn is blocked. Black\'s other pawn is on b5. White king is on a3, which is close to a-file.\n\n- Black might play b5-b4, but White can play Kb4, capturing the pawn.\n\n- So Black loses the b-pawn, and a-pawn is blocked. Not good.\n\nOption 2: b5-b4.\n\n- White responds with Kb3 (best move to block).\n\n- Now b-pawn is blocked. Black plays a5-a4.\n\n- White has to decide: go after a4 or stay on b3.\n\n- If White plays Kc4, then Black\'s b-pawn is still blocked, but a-pawn advances.\n\n- Or White takes on a4: Kxa4, then Black plays b4-b3, and White king is on a4, which is far from b3.\n\n- After Kxa4, Black plays b4-b3, then White king needs to go from a4 to b3, which takes two moves (a4-b4-b3), but Black pawn promotes in two more moves (b3-b2-b1).\n\nLet\'s see:\n\n1...b4\n\n2. Kb3 a4\n\n3. Kxa4 b3\n\n4. Kb4 b2\n\n5. Kc3 b1=Q\n\nSo Black promotes.\n\nWait, let\'s step through:\n\n